“I will design a simplified e-commerce system like Amazon or Flipkart that allows users to browse products, add items to a cart, and place orders.”

Functional Requirements :-
User can browse/search products
User can view product details
User can add/remove items from cart
User can place an order
User can view order status

Non-Functional Requirements :-
Performance
Availability
Scalability
Consistency (especially inventory)
Reliability

Browsing Product.
“When a user browses products, the request goes through the API Gateway to the Product or Search Service, which fetches product metadata from the catalog store.
Frequently accessed product data is cached to ensure low-latency responses.”

Adding items in a Cart.
“When a user adds an item to the cart, the request goes to the Cart Service. The cart is maintained per user, and the cart data is usually stored in an 
in-memory store like Redis for fast access. Each user has an independent cart that can be updated by adding or removing items.”

Placing the Order.
“When a user places an order, the Order Service first creates a pending order and requests the Inventory Service to reserve the required stock. Once the stock 
is successfully reserved, the Payment 
Service processes the payment. If payment succeeds, the order is marked as confirmed and the reserved inventory is deducted.”
“Overselling is avoided by reserving inventory before payment and using atomic inventory updates to ensure only available stock can be purchased.”
✅ Option 1: Inventory Reservation (Most Common)
Temporarily reserve stock when order starts
Reservation has a TTL
If payment fails → release stock

✅ Option 2: Atomic Update / Lock (Mention briefly)
Inventory decrement is atomic
Only one request succeeds
Don’t go deep unless asked.

Scaling the System.
The system is made scalable by horizontally scaling stateless services and using database sharding. Product catalog data, which is read-heavy, can be stored in a NoSQL key-value or document store and 
heavily cached using Redis and CDNs for fast access. Images are served via CDNs.Critical workflows like orders and inventory use strongly consistent storage, while non-critical tasks are handled asynchronously.
“Cache and CDN for reads, sharding for writes, stateless services for scale.”

Durability/Reliabilty
“Writes in an e-commerce system include order creation and inventory updates, which are scaled using database sharding. Read-heavy operations like product browsing are optimized using caching, and static content 
is served via CDNs. Stateless services are critical for scalability because they allow requests to be handled by any server instance, enabling easy horizontal scaling and fault tolerance.”

“Durability is ensured by persisting critical data like orders and payments to reliable databases before acknowledging success. Reliability is achieved using idempotency to avoid duplicates, inventory reservation to 
prevent overselling, asynchronous processing via queues to isolate failures, database replication for fault tolerance, and graceful failure handling with retries and state transitions.”

